@meta
id: 21-11
title: The macro that calls itself
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-recursive
par: 72
minutes: 8
vscode: a regex replace with three groups, once you have worked out the escaping
open: customers.fixture.ts

@brief
The customer fixture came out of a wiki table, one customer per line,
fields separated by pipes. Each becomes a record:

    cus_1|Alice Anderson|alice.anderson@example.com
    { id: 'cus_1', name: 'Alice Anderson', email: 'alice.anderson@example.com' },

Twenty-four lines and then `];`.

You could count them, or throw `99@a` at it. There is a third way that
needs no number at all: a macro whose last keystroke is `@a`. It plays
itself, which plays itself, until something fails. This lesson is
about how to record one without it running away from you.

@keys
| `qaq` | record nothing into `a`, which empties it |
| `qa` … `@a` `q` | record, ending with a call to the macro itself |
| `@a` | run it; it stops when something fails |

@hints
Empty the register first with `qaq`. While you are recording, the `@a`
at the end plays an empty register, which does nothing, so you can
record calmly on one line and stop.
--
The guard comes first: `^f|` fails on `];`, which has no pipe, and
that failure is what ends the loop. Then `s', name: '` `Esc`, `f|` and
`s', email: '` `Esc`, `^i{ id: '` `Esc`, `A' },` `Esc`, `j`, and
finally `@a` before the `q`.
--
`j` `qaq` then `qa^f|s', name: '<Esc>f|s', email: '<Esc>^i{ id: '<Esc>A' },<Esc>j@aq`, then `@a`.

@solution
    j  qaq   qa ^f| s', name: ' <Esc> f| s', email: ' <Esc> ^i{ id: ' <Esc> A' }, <Esc> j @a q   @a   :wq<Enter>

Sixty-two keystrokes, and the run was one `@a`.

Three moving parts. `qaq` is a recording that contains nothing, and it
matters: if register `a` still held last week's macro, the `@a` you
type inside the new recording would play the old one, in the middle of
the new one, on the line you are looking at. Empty it first, every
time.

The `@a` at the end is a call to the macro itself. While recording, it
calls an empty register and nothing happens. When you play the
finished macro it calls the finished macro, which calls it again, and
the only way out is for a keystroke to fail. That is the same safety
property as the count, with the count removed. The macro runs until
the data runs out.

Which means the guard is not optional. `^f|` is the first thing the
macro does on every line, and on `];` it fails, and the loop ends
there, before anything on that line is changed. Put the guard first
and you can stop thinking about how many lines there are. Forget the
guard, and the loop reaches the end of the file and stops only when
`j` fails, with every line in between edited.

If it does run away, `Ctrl-C` stops it and `u` puts the file back.

